        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Ascent Whey
          </h2>

          <p>
            The chocolate whey label lists a whey protein isolate blend, made of native whey protein isolate and whey protein isolate, followed by whey protein concentrate, cocoa processed with alkali, natural flavors, lecithin, sea salt and stevia leaf extract. Vanilla is the same minus the cocoa. The label states that it contains milk and soy, and the soy comes from the lecithin, which helps the powder mix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Per scoop, chocolate has 120 calories, 1 g of fat, 4 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of sugar with no added sugar, and 25 g of protein. Vanilla has 2 g of carbohydrate in a slightly smaller 31 g scoop. Ascent also lists 5.5 g of BCAAs and 2.6 g of leucine per scoop. That leucine figure is on the high side for 25 g of whey and comfortably above the roughly 2-3 g per meal that research associates with a strong muscle-building response.
          </p>

          <p>
            So what does native whey actually mean? Most whey is a by-product of cheesemaking. Native whey is filtered directly from milk before any cheese is made, so it skips the heat and the cheese cultures. Brands argue this keeps the protein less processed and gives a slightly higher leucine content. The evidence that this produces meaningfully better results than good conventional whey is thin. Treat it as a quality marker, not a performance upgrade.
          </p>

          <p>
            The concentrate is the part to notice if you are sensitive to lactose. Concentrate keeps more lactose and fat than isolate. The small amount in Ascent's blend will not bother most people, but if regular whey bloats you, a pure isolate is the safer pick. Our guide to <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> explains the difference, and the Transparent Labs card above is the pure-isolate alternative.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">CLEAN LABEL CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">What Ascent leaves out</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">No sucralose, acesulfame potassium, aspartame or artificial flavors appear on Ascent's whey, casein or clear whey labels at the time of writing. Ascent also lists its products as rBST and rBGH free. The clear whey is the exception on sugar: it contains organic cane sugar, about 1 g of added sugar per scoop.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="casein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Why the Casein Is the Standout
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ascent's Micellar Casein is the product in the range that beats most of its direct competition. A 36 g scoop of chocolate gives 25 g of protein and 120 calories, with 1 g of fat, 4 g of carbohydrate including 1 g of fiber, and 1 g of sugar. It is listed as micellar casein isolate, which is a cleaner protein base than many casein blends. It is soy-free, unlike the whey, and Informed Sport certified.
          </p>

          <p>
            Casein digests slowly, releasing amino acids over several hours, which is why it is sold as a bedtime protein. Research on pre-sleep protein suggests around 30-40 g of casein before bed can support overnight muscle protein synthesis, so a heaping scoop or a scoop plus some Greek yogurt is a reasonable target. Ascent lists 2.2 g of leucine and 10.7 g of essential amino acids per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            The calcium is a useful bonus. Casein carries calcium naturally, and the chocolate label lists about 661 mg, roughly 50% of the daily value. That is worth knowing if you avoid other dairy. It is also worth counting if you take a calcium supplement. Talk to your doctor or a dietitian if you have kidney stones or are on medication that interacts with calcium.
          </p>

          <p>
            At list price, a 2 lb bag of about 25 servings costs around $45, which works out to roughly $1.80 per 25 g of protein. That makes it the cheapest product in the Ascent range per gram and competitive with other certified caseins. Our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> guide ranks it against the rest of the category.
          </p>
        </div>

